@[expose] public section

/-! # Tilings of grids, cylinders, and tori

Statement formalizations of the fixed-point formulas in Kagey and Keehn.  Tile actions are
encoded one group element at a time by a permutation `σ`; this is slightly more general than
choosing an ambient dihedral-set action because every proof in the cited formulas uses only the
cyclic subgroup generated by that element.
-/

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of the reflection `f` on an `n × m` grid:
`(x,y) ↦ (n-1-x,y)`.  In `ZMod n`, `n-1-x` is represented by `-1-x`; the second coordinate is
unchanged.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellF (n m : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod m) : ZMod n × ZMod m :=
  (-1 - c.1, c.2)

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of the half-turn `r²` on an `n × m` grid:
`(x,y) ↦ (n-1-x,m-1-y)`, with both modular subtractions represented by `-1`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellR2 (n m : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod m) : ZMod n × ZMod m :=
  (-1 - c.1, -1 - c.2)

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of `r²f` on an `n × m` grid:
`(x,y) ↦ (x,m-1-y)`.  The first coordinate is unchanged and modular subtraction is `-1-y`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellR2F (n m : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod m) : ZMod n × ZMod m :=
  (c.1, -1 - c.2)

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of the quarter-turn `r` on an `n × n` grid:
`(x,y) ↦ (n-1-y,x)`.  The common modulus of both coordinates records that this is square-only.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellR (n : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod n) : ZMod n × ZMod n :=
  (-1 - c.2, c.1)

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of `r³` on an `n × n` grid:
`(x,y) ↦ (y,n-1-x)`, using `-1-x` for modular subtraction.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellR3 (n : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod n) : ZMod n × ZMod n :=
  (c.2, -1 - c.1)

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of the diagonal reflection `rf` on an `n × n`
grid: `(x,y) ↦ (y,x)`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellRF (n : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod n) : ZMod n × ZMod n :=
  (c.2, c.1)

/-- The cell map for the paper's right action of the other diagonal reflection `r³f` on an
`n × n` grid: `(x,y) ↦ (n-1-y,n-1-x)`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def cellR3F (n : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod n) : ZMod n × ZMod n :=
  (-1 - c.2, -1 - c.1)

/-- The number of tilings `F : α → T` fixed by a cell map `φ` and tile permutation `σ`.
The subtype equation is exactly the paper's condition `F (c·s) = F c·g`, with `φ` encoding
the cell part and `σ` encoding the design part.  This definition makes no ambient group-action
assumption because a single symmetry is sufficient for every fixed-point formula below.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def fixedTilingCount {α T : Type*} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]
    [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T] (φ : α → α) (σ : Equiv.Perm T) : ℕ :=
  Fintype.card {F : α → T // ∀ c, F (φ c) = σ (F c)}

/-- The formal counterpart of `t_{g^k}`: the cardinality of the subtype of tile designs fixed
by `σ^k`.  In particular, exponent zero represents `t_id`, while exponents one and two represent
`t_g` and `t_{g²}`.  The explicit fixed-point subtype avoids assuming a global action on `T`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public def tileFixedCount {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (k : ℕ) : ℕ :=
  Fintype.card {d : T // (σ ^ k) d = d}

/-- The finite orbit of `c` under nonnegative powers of the cell permutation `φ`.  Because `φ`
is a permutation of a finite type, these are precisely the cyclic-subgroup orbits.  This explicit
encoding retains fixed one-cycles, unlike `Equiv.Perm.cycleType`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 20. -/
public noncomputable def permOrbit {α : Type*} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]
    (φ : Equiv.Perm α) (c : α) : Finset α := by
  classical
  exact Finset.univ.filter fun d => ∃ k : ℕ, (φ ^ k) c = d

/-- The duplicate-free finite set of all cyclic orbits of `φ`, obtained by mapping every cell to
its orbit and using `Finset.image` to identify repetitions.  Singleton fixed-point orbits remain
present, which is required by Theorem 20.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 20. -/
public noncomputable def permOrbits {α : Type*} [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α]
    (φ : Equiv.Perm α) : Finset (Finset α) := by
  classical
  exact Finset.univ.image (permOrbit φ)

/-- The sum over cylinder shifts `a ∈ ZMod n` of fixed tilings for `(a,g)`.  The shifted cell
`(x+a,y)` is fed to `cellG`, so this follows the paper's “shift first, then apply `g`” right-action
convention.  `σ` is the action of `g` on tile designs.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public noncomputable def cylinderShiftSum {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m]
    (cellG : ZMod n × ZMod m → ZMod n × ZMod m) (σ : Equiv.Perm T) : ℕ :=
  ∑ a : ZMod n,
    fixedTilingCount (fun c => cellG (c.1 + a, c.2)) σ

/-- The sum over torus shifts `(a,b) ∈ ZMod n × ZMod m` of fixed tilings for `((a,b),g)`.
Both coordinates are shifted before `cellG` is applied, while shifts act trivially on tile
designs and `σ` records only the action of `g`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public noncomputable def torusShiftSum {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m]
    (cellG : ZMod n × ZMod m → ZMod n × ZMod m) (σ : Equiv.Perm T) : ℕ :=
  ∑ a : ZMod n, ∑ b : ZMod m,
    fixedTilingCount (fun c => cellG (c.1 + a, c.2 + b)) σ

/-- Applying the cell reflection `f` twice returns every cell to itself.  This is the
characteristic involution fact for the new definition `cellF`; the equality is coordinatewise in
the two modular rings and does not require a positivity assumption on either modulus.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Setting. -/
public theorem cellF_involutive (n m : ℕ) (c : ZMod n × ZMod m) :
    cellF n m (cellF n m c) = c := by
  ext <;> simp [cellF]

/-- On two cells with two tile designs, the identity cell map and identity tile permutation fix
all four maps.  This concrete `decide` proof is a characteristic sanity check for
`fixedTilingCount`, not one of the paper's enumerative theorems.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Encoding check. -/
public theorem fixedTilingCount_fin_two_identity :
    fixedTilingCount (α := Fin 2) (T := Fin 2) id 1 = 4 := by
  decide +kernel

/-- On the `2 × 1` grid, reflecting the two cells while swapping the two tile designs fixes
exactly two tilings.  The proof by `decide` checks the interaction of a nontrivial cell map and a
nontrivial design permutation in `fixedTilingCount`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Encoding check. -/
public theorem fixedTilingCount_cellF_two_one_swap :
    fixedTilingCount (cellF 2 1) (Equiv.swap (0 : Fin 2) 1) = 2 := by
  decide +kernel

/-- For a finite cell permutation `φ`, the number of fixed tilings is the product over its
cyclic orbits `θ` of the number of designs fixed by `σ ^ |θ|`.  `permOrbits` explicitly includes
fixed one-cycles, addressing the omission of one-cycles from `Equiv.Perm.cycleType`.  Quantifying
over an arbitrary `σ` generalizes the paper's `R`-set formulation to every action of the single
element `g`; no order hypothesis is needed for this general orbit formula.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 20. -/
public theorem_wanted fixedTilingCount_eq_orbit_product {α T : Type*}
    [Fintype α] [DecidableEq α] [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (φ : Equiv.Perm α) (σ : Equiv.Perm T) :
    fixedTilingCount φ σ =
      ∏ θ ∈ permOrbits φ, tileFixedCount σ θ.card

/-- The identity fixes every rectangular-grid tiling, giving `|T|^(nm)`.  The hypothesis
`σ^1=1` records that `σ` represents the identity element, and thus this quantification covers the
identity action in every `R`-set.  The Burnside conclusion of Theorem 22 is intentionally not part
of this single-element fixed-point statement.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_identity {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 1 = 1) :
    fixedTilingCount (id : ZMod n × ZMod m → ZMod n × ZMod m) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ (n * m)

/-- For an even number `nm` of rectangular-grid cells, the half-turn `r²` has only two-cycles,
so its fixed-tiling count is `|T|^(nm/2)`.  `σ²=1` is the required order-two hypothesis and the
statement applies to every corresponding `R`-set action.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_r2_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1)
    (heven : Even (n * m)) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2 n m) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n * m / 2)

/-- For odd `nm`, the half-turn `r²` has one fixed cell and `(nm-1)/2` two-cycles.  The factor
`tileFixedCount σ 1` is `t_{r²}` and `σ²=1` records the element's order; this covers every action
of `r²` on the tile set.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_r2_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1)
    (hodd : Odd (n * m)) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2 n m) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ ((n * m - 1) / 2) * tileFixedCount σ 1

/-- When `n` is even, reflection `f` has no fixed columns and its rectangular-grid fixed count is
`|T|^(nm/2)`.  The order-two hypothesis on `σ` represents `f` in any `R`-set.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_f_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1)
    (heven : Even n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellF n m) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n * m / 2)

/-- When `n` is odd, reflection `f` has `m` fixed cells and `m(n-1)/2` two-cycles.  Their design
factor is `t_f^m`, encoded as `(tileFixedCount σ 1)^m`, and `σ²=1` is the appropriate order
hypothesis for every `R`-set action.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_f_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1)
    (hodd : Odd n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellF n m) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ (m * (n - 1) / 2) * tileFixedCount σ 1 ^ m

/-- When `m` is even, reflection `r²f` has no fixed rows, giving `|T|^(nm/2)`.  The hypothesis
`σ²=1` expresses the reflection's order on tile designs and quantifies over every such action.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_r2f_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1)
    (heven : Even m) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2F n m) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n * m / 2)

/-- When `m` is odd, reflection `r²f` has `n` fixed cells and `n(m-1)/2` two-cycles.  The fixed
design factor is `(tileFixedCount σ 1)^n`; `σ²=1` is the reflection order hypothesis.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 22. -/
public theorem_wanted rectangularGrid_r2f_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n m : ℕ) [NeZero n] [NeZero m] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1)
    (hodd : Odd m) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2F n m) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ (n * (m - 1) / 2) * tileFixedCount σ 1 ^ n

/-- The square-grid identity formula is `|T|^(n²)`.  `σ^1=1` encodes the identity action on
designs for every `D₈`-set; the Burnside conclusion in the paper is omitted because no ambient
dihedral action is introduced here.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_identity {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 1 = 1) :
    fixedTilingCount (id : ZMod n × ZMod n → ZMod n × ZMod n) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ (n ^ 2)

/-- For even `n`, the square-grid half-turn has only two-cycles, producing `|T|^(n²/2)`.
The tile permutation satisfies `σ²=1`, as required for `r²` in every `D₈`-set.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r2_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1) (heven : Even n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2 n n) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n ^ 2 / 2)

/-- For odd `n`, the square-grid half-turn has one fixed cell.  The factor
`tileFixedCount σ 1` is `t_{r²}`, and `σ²=1` is the element-order hypothesis.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r2_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1) (hodd : Odd n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2 n n) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ ((n ^ 2 - 1) / 2) * tileFixedCount σ 1

/-- For even `n`, either axial square-grid reflection `f` has no fixed column and the count is
`|T|^(n²/2)`.  Here the statement is for `f`, with `σ²=1` encoding its action on designs.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_f_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1) (heven : Even n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellF n n) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n ^ 2 / 2)

/-- For odd `n`, `f` has `n` fixed cells and `(n²-n)/2` two-cycles.  Its fixed-design factor is
`(tileFixedCount σ 1)^n`, and `σ²=1` covers every order-two tile action.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_f_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1) (hodd : Odd n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellF n n) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ ((n ^ 2 - n) / 2) * tileFixedCount σ 1 ^ n

/-- For even `n`, the `r²f` square-grid reflection has count `|T|^(n²/2)`.  The hypothesis
`σ²=1` is its order condition on tile designs.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r2f_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1) (heven : Even n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2F n n) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n ^ 2 / 2)

/-- For odd `n`, `r²f` has `n` fixed cells and `(n²-n)/2` two-cycles.  The `t_{r²f}` factor is
`tileFixedCount σ 1`, and the permutation is assumed to square to the identity.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r2f_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 2 = 1) (hodd : Odd n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR2F n n) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ ((n ^ 2 - n) / 2) * tileFixedCount σ 1 ^ n

/-- For even `n`, the quarter-turn `r` decomposes the square grid into four-cycles, giving
`|T|^(n²/4)`.  The hypothesis `σ⁴=1` is the required order-four condition for every tile action
of `r`.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 4 = 1) (heven : Even n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR n) σ = Fintype.card T ^ (n ^ 2 / 4)

/-- For odd `n`, the quarter-turn `r` has one fixed cell and `(n²-1)/4` four-cycles.  The fixed
cell contributes `tileFixedCount σ 1 = t_r`, and `σ⁴=1` records the rotation order.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r_odd {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 4 = 1) (hodd : Odd n) :
    fixedTilingCount (cellR n) σ =
      Fintype.card T ^ ((n ^ 2 - 1) / 4) * tileFixedCount σ 1

/-- The `r³` formula for even `n` is the same four-cycle count as for `r`.  It is stated
separately with its own order-four tile permutation, so it directly covers the action of `r³` in
every `D₈`-set.

Formalization deviation: none beyond the representation choices stated above.

Source: Peter Kagey, William Keehn, *Counting Tilings of the n × m Grid, Cylinder, and Torus*,
        Journal of Integer Sequences 27 (2024), Article 24.6.1, Theorem 24. -/
public theorem_wanted squareGrid_r3_even {T : Type*} [Fintype T] [DecidableEq T]
    (n : ℕ) [NeZero n] (σ : Equiv.Perm T) (hσ : σ ^ 4 = 1) (heven : Even n) :
